          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em Planck’s constant
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (after
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+unit"} physical unit
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"}> action
            ” which sets the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
            at which effects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            are genuinely important and physics is no longer well approximated by
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
          %p This is discussed in the section:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#AsAPhysicalConstant"} As a physical constant
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematical
            formulation of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in \mathbb{R}^\times
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\stackrel{h\cdot(-)}{\longrightarrow} \mathbb{R} \to U(1)
            which governs the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            lift from real (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"}> differential
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"}> differential
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integrality
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            here is the very “quantum”-ness of quantum theory, and this is what Planck’s constant parameterizes. This is discussed in the section:
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#InGeometricQuantization"} In geometric quantization
              \.
          %p
            Finally, when infinitesimally approximating this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            step in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            (see at
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal expansion parameter
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#InFormalDeformationQuantization"} In perturbative quantization
              \.
          %p
            Applied to the key example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            it turns out that the powers of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            in contributions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            essentially correspond to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"} loop order
            of the given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InFeynmanPerturbationSeries"} In the Feynman perturbation series
          %h2#AsAPhysicalConstant As a physical constant
          %p
            The original
            %strong Planck constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is a quantum of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            by the fact that each of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quanta"} quanta
            – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
            – carries an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            that is fixed by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequency"} frequency
            (cycles per second)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            according to the relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = h\nu
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/laser"} laser
            beam of fixed frequency
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            is an integer multiple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mi h
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n h \nu
            of a packet of energy
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\nu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is the number of photons emitted.
          %p
            As a fundamental physical constant,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            has dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi mass
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi length
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi time
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (mass)(length)^2(time)^{-1}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+units"} units
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo =
                  %mn 6.62607015
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 34
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %mi kg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h = 6.62607015 \cdot 10^{-34} m^2 kg / s
          %p by the SI definition of the kilogram.
          %p
            The
            %em reduced Planck constant
            or
            %em Dirac constant
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Dirac33"} Dirac 1933
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/pdf/Proceedings_Physics/1933.pdf#page=235"} p. 208
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar \,\coloneqq\, h/2\pi
          %p
            is the proportionality constant that relates energy (of a photon) to angular frequency
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            (radians per second as opposed to cycles per second), so that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = \hbar \omega
          %h2#InGeometricQuantization In geometric quantization
          %h3#BasicDefinition Basic definition
          %p
            The step of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            is about refining data in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"}> differential
            ) real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"}> differential
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            as an addiditve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            on which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            is chosen as part of the identification of mathematical formalism with physical reality, one should really consider
            %em all
            possible additive group homomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  h \in (\mathbb{R}- \{0\}) \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi h
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-)\cdot h \;\colon\; \mathbb{Z} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            and this “physical
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is what is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Planck’s constant
          %p
            In particular the induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            is identified as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \mathbb{Z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi h
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \simeq \mathbb{R}/h \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            and under this identification its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            map is expressed in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+function"} exponential function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi z
                  %mo ↦
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo !
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp \colon z \mapsto  \sum_{k = 0}^\infty \frac{z^k}{k!} \in \mathbb{C}
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp\big(
                  2 \pi \tfrac{\mathrm{i}}{h}(-)
                  \\big)
                  \\;=\;
                  \\exp\big(
                  \\tfrac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} (-)
                  \\big)
                  \\;\colon\; \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow U(1)
                  \\,,
          %p where
          #eq:ReducedPlanckViaPrequantization.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar \,\coloneqq\, h/2\pi
          %p
            is the “reduced Planck constant”, or
            %em Dirac constant
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Dirac33"} Dirac 1933
            = succeed "&rbrack;." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/pdf/Proceedings_Physics/1933.pdf#page=235"} p. 208
          %p
            The resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            is the real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+exact+sequence"} exponential exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mi i
                            %mi ℏ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0
                  \\to
                  \\mathbb{Z}
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  \\stackrel{\exp\left(\tfrac{i}{\hbar}(-)\right)}{\longrightarrow}
                  U(1)
                  \\to
                  0
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the source of the ubiquity of the expression
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} (-))
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            appears as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} S)
          %h3#in_relation_to_the_symplectic_form In relation to the symplectic form
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            Planck’s constant appears as the inverse scale of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
          %p
            For instance in the simple case that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* \mathbb{R} \simeq \mathbb{R}^2
            with standard coordinates
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p,q\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi d
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi dq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim d p \wedge dq
            actually needed in physics is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi ℏ
                  %mi d
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega = \frac{1}{\hbar} d p \wedge d q
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is because after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of this form the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            will obey
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mover
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\hat q, \hat p] = i (\omega_{p,q})^{-1}
          %p and this is supposed to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots = i \hbar
                  \\,.
          %p
            Accordingly, it follows that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \nabla)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            with itself, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^{\otimes k}, \nabla_k)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \omega
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar \to \hbar / k
                  \\,.
          %p This implies in particular
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a global rescaling of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
                of the symplectic form may be absorbed in a rescaling of Planck’s constant, see at
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+non-integral+forms"} geometric quantization of non-integral forms
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ∇
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E, \nabla)
                a given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
                the limit of the tensor powers
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi E
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mo ∇
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^{\otimes k}, \nabla_k)
                as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                tends to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity"} infinity
                roughly corresponds to taking a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+limit"} classical limit
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Donaldson00"} Donaldson 00
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            Planck’s constant corresponds to the inverse
            %em level
            of the theory, hence the inverse of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            that defines the theory, regarded as an element in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            Similarly for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            Chern-Simons theory may be taken to have as the fundamental value
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Pontryagin+class"} first Pontryagin class
            that defines the theory is divisible by 2, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+n-bundle"} prequantum 3-bundle
            that defines the theory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"} principal connections
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi Spin
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 3
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tfrac{1}{2}\hat \mathbf{p}_1 : \mathbf{B}Spin_{conn} \to \mathbf{B}^3 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Similarly for 7-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+2-group"} String 2-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            the fundamental value is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 6
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 6
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi String
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mn 7
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tfrac{1}{6}\hat \mathbf{p}_2 : \mathbf{B}String_{conn} \to \mathbf{B}^7 U(1)_{conn}
                  \\,.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            for more on this.
          %h2#InFormalDeformationQuantization In perturbative quantization
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            Planck’s constant (together with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            are expressed as (generally non-converging,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the coupling constant.
          %p
            This is explicitly realized by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            as as deformation of the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            to a non-commutative algebra on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            the original observables.
          %h2#in_perturbative_quantum_field_theory In perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            are expressed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            and) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %em loop order
            refers to something like the “number of loops” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            that contibutes to a given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            that is contributed by this diagram (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RelationToPowersInPlancksConstant"} below
          %p
            Therefore contributions of graphs at zero without loops (these are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trees"} trees
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar \to 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            solutions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Helling"} Helling
          %p
            Most predictions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            have very good agreement with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            already to very low loop order, first or second; inclusion of third loop order is used (at least in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cacciari05"} Cacciari 05, slide 5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            computation, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ADDHM15"} ADDHM 15
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moments"} anomalous magnetic moments
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AHKN12"} AHKN 12
          %p This usefulness of low loop order is forturnate because
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
                for all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
                of interest has
                %em vanishing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
                (
                %a{:href => "perturbation+theory#Dyson52"}> Dyson 52
                ), hence is at best an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
                for which the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
                of more than some low order terms is meaningless;
            %li
              %p the computational effort increases immensely with loop order.
          %h2#InFeynmanPerturbationSeries In the Feynman perturbation series
          %p
            In the computation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+matrix"} scattering matrix
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"}> Feynman perturbation series
            ) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            and)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            whose contributions may be labeled by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is a finite labeled
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            to which this graph contributes is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar^{ E(\Gamma) - V(\Gamma) }
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(\Gamma) \in \mathbb{N}
                is the number of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
                of the graph
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\Gamma) \in \mathbb{N}
                is the number of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
                in the graph.
          %p
            This comes about (see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "S-matrix#ExistenceAndRenormalization"} S-matrix – Feynman diagrams and Renormalization
            for details) because
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the explicit
                = succeed "-dependence" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
                is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mi g
                            %mi ℏ
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi int
                        %mo )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi g
                            %mi k
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mi k
                          %mi k
                          %mo !
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi L
                              %mi int
                            %mi ⋯
                            %msub
                              %mi L
                              %mi int
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mtext factors
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      S\left(\tfrac{g}{\hbar} L_{int} \right)
                      \=
                      \\underset{k \in \mathbb{N}}{\sum} \frac{g^k}{\hbar^k k!} T( \underset{k \, \text{factors}}{\underbrace{L_{int} \cdots L_{int}}} )
            %li
              %p
                the further
                = succeed "-dependence" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\cdots)
                is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi int
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi prod
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo ∫
                        %msub
                          %mi ω
                          %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mfrac
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mfrac
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi int
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      T(L_{int} L_{int}) = prod \circ \exp\left( \hbar \int \omega_{F}(x,y) \frac{\delta}{\delta \phi(x)} \otimes \frac{\delta}{\delta \phi(y)}  \right) ( L_{int} \otimes L_{int} )
                      \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_F
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x)
            the field observable at point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            (where we are notationally suppressing the internal degrees of freedom of the fields for simplicity, writing them as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            powers).
          %p The resulting terms of the S-matrix series are thus labeled by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the number of factors of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi int
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{int}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
                of the corresponding Feynman diagram and hence each contibute with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar^{-1}
            %li
              %p
                the number of integrals over the Feynman propagator
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ω
                          %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_F
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℏ
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar^1
          %p
            Now the formula for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic+of+planar+graphs"} Euler characteristic of planar graphs
            says that the number of regions in a plane that are encircled by edges, the
            %em faces
            here thought of as the number of “loops”, is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L(\Gamma) =  1 + E(\Gamma) - V(\Gamma)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence a planar Feynman diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            contributes with the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+order"}> loop order
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(\Gamma)
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar^{L(\Gamma)-1}
                  \\,.
          %p So far this is the discussion for internal edges. An actual scattering matrix element is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi out
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi S
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi g
                        %mi ℏ
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi int
                    %mo )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi in
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle \psi_{out} \vert S\left(\tfrac{g}{\hbar} L_{int} \right)
                  \\vert \psi_{in} \rangle
                  \\,,
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi in
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∝
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi n
                                %mi in
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi in
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi vac
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vert \psi_{in}\rangle
                  \\propto
                  \\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{\hbar^{n_{in}}}}
                  \\phi^\dagger(k_1) \cdots \phi^\dagger(k_{n_{in}}) \vert vac \rangle
          %p
            is a state of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi in
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in}
            free field quanta and similarly
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi out
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ∝
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi n
                                %mi out
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ⋯
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi out
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi vac
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vert \psi_{out}\rangle
                  \\propto
                  \\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{\hbar^{n_{out}}}}
                  \\phi^\dagger(k_1) \cdots \phi^\dagger(k_{n_{out}}) \vert vac \rangle
          %p
            is a state of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi out
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out}
            field quanta. The normalization of these states, in view of the commutation relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo †
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∝
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\phi(k), \phi^\dagger(q)] \propto \hbar
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
          %p
            This means that an actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{ext}(\Gamma)
            external vertices scales as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi ext
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar^{L(\Gamma) - 1 + E_{ext}(\Gamma)/2 }
                  \\,.
          %p
            (For the analogous discussion of the dependence on the actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bogoliubov%27s+formula"} Bogoliubov's formula
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "Bogoliubov's+formula#PowersInPlancksConstant"} there
          %h2#History History
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
            introduced the constant named after him in the discussion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+body+radiation"} black body radiation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            black body radiation comes out completely wrong (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+catastrophe"}> ultraviolet catastrophe
            ”). Planck fixed this in an ad-hoc but succesful manner by postulating that
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequency"} frequency
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/harmonic+oscillators"} harmonic oscillators
            in the black body (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atoms"}> atoms
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/molecules"} molecules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory (physics)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                fundamental
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scales"} scales
              (fundamental/
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+unit"} natural
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+units"} physical units
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/speed+of+light"} speed of light
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℏ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+constant"} gravitational constant
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi κ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 8
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_N = \kappa^2/8\pi
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+length"} Planck length
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ℓ
                              %mi p
                            %mo =
                            %msqrt
                              %mrow
                                %mi ℏ
                                %mi G
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %msup
                                  %mi c
                                  %mn 3
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell_p = \sqrt{ \hbar G / c^3 }
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+mass"} Planck mass
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi m
                              %mi p
                            %mo =
                            %msqrt
                              %mrow
                                %mi ℏ
                                %mi c
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_p = \sqrt{\hbar c / G}
              %li
                %p
                  depending on a given
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Compton+wavelength"} Compton wavelength
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi λ
                              %mi m
                            %mo =
                            %mi ℏ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi m
                            %mi c
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_m = \hbar / m c
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwarzschild+radius"} Schwarzschild radius
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi m
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %msup
                              %mi c
                              %mn 2
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 m G / c^2
                  %li
                    %p
                      depending also on a given
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi e
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                    %ul
                      %li
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger+limit"} Schwinger limit
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi E
                                %mi crit
                              %mo =
                              %msup
                                %mi m
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mi c
                                %mn 3
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi e
                              %mi ℏ
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{crit} = m^2 c^3 /  e \hbar
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT+scale"} GUT scale
              %li
                %p
                  %a{:href => "string+theory#Scales"} string scale
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+tension"} string tension
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi T
                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 2
                            %mi π
                            %msup
                              %mi α
                              %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                                %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = 1/(2\pi \alpha^\prime)
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+length+scale"} string length scale
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ℓ
                              %mi s
                            %mo =
                            %msqrt
                              %mrow
                                %mi α
                                %mo ′
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell_s = \sqrt{\alpha'}
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mi s
                            %mo =
                            %msup
                              %mi e
                              %mi λ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_s = e^\lambda
          %h2#References References
          %p Planck’s constant originates in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
              (transl. M. Martius),
              %a{:href => "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/40030/40030-pdf.pdf#page=178"} p. 164
              in:
              %em The Theory of Heat Radiation
              (1914) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/40030/40030-pdf.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DiracPrinciples-DiracConstant.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Dirac30"} Dirac 1930, p. 87
          %p
            The notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            for the reduced Planck constant
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ReducedPlanckViaPrequantization"} (1)
            is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Dirac30
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Dirac"}> Paul Dirac
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Principles+of+Quantum+Mechanics"} The Principles of Quantum Mechanics
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-principles-of-quantum-mechanics-9780198520115"} ISBN:9780198520115
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DiracIntroducingHBar.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Dirac33"} Dirac 1933, p. 208
          %p but Dirac did not stick to this notation in his publications, until:
          %ul
            %li#Dirac33
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+A.+M.+Dirac"}> Paul A. M. Dirac
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/pdf/Proceedings_Physics/1933.pdf#page=235"} p. 208
              in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Théorie du Positron
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structure et propriétés des noyaux atomiques
              %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/"} Solvay
              Conference (1933) 203-212 &amp;lbrack;full:
              %a{:href => "http://www.solvayinstitutes.be/pdf/Proceedings_Physics/1933.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Further discussion may be found in any monograph on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            (see references
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "quantum+mechanics#References"} there
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Donaldson00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Donaldson"}> Simon Donaldson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Planck’s constant in complex and almost-complex geometry
              %p 2000), 63–72, Int. Press, Boston, MA, 2001
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_constant"} Planck’s constant
